no(201,'The expected terminal alkyne is C (but-1-yne), not old B (internal but-2-yne). Supplied formulas have missing triple bonds and inconsistent valencies; matching Myschool14551 also has corrupted formulas. Restore all four exact structures from a sound original rather than infer each missing bond from the desired answer.',answer='C if restored to but-1-yne')
yes(202,'A','Branched alkanes generally resist premature autoignition better than corresponding straight-chain alkanes. This increases knock resistance, allowing a petrol engine to operate more effectively at high compression.',['https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/gc/d2gc01200f'],question='Increasing which component generally improves the knock resistance of petrol in a high-compression internal combustion engine?',options=['Branched-chain alkanes','Straight-chain alkanes','Cycloalkanes','Halogenated hydrocarbons'],audit='Recovered B merged into A and corrected spelling. Explained the specific knock-resistance property rather than claiming universal energy-content improvement.')
yes(203,'B','Ethanol gives the iodoform reaction with alkaline iodine/hypoiodite. The yellow precipitate is CHI₃, called triiodomethane or iodoform. The other listed compounds are not the yellow solid formed in this test.',['https://edu.rsc.org/experiments/a-test-to-distinguish-between-ethanol-and-methanol/548.article'],question='Ethanol reacts with aqueous sodium hypoiodite to give a yellow solid with a characteristic smell. What is the solid?',options=['Trichloromethane','Triiodomethane','Iodoethane','Ethanal'],audit='Restored hypoiodite spelling/name and triiodomethane from malformed option B; reagent identity preserved.')
yes(204,'D','Methane, ethane and propane are small hydrocarbons with very low boiling points. They belong to the light gaseous fraction. Petrol, kerosene and benzene boil at higher temperatures and are not all part of the most volatile fraction.',[ORG],question='Which set contains only components of the most volatile fraction obtained from crude petroleum?',options=['Butane, propane and kerosene','Butane, propane and petrol','Ethane, methane and benzene','Ethane, methane and propane'])
yes(205,'C','The aqueous ash extract supplies alkaline substances. These react with fats in palm oil during saponification to form fatty-acid salts, which are soap. The palm oil supplies the fat, while the ash supplies the alkali.',['https://edu.rsc.org/exhibition-chemistry/soap-from-bacon-the-dangers-of-alkaline-solutions/2020068.article','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry?page=244&qtid=813440'],question='Local black soap is made by boiling palm oil with an aqueous extract of ash. What does the ash principally provide?',options=['Acid','An alkanoic-acid ester','Alkali','Alkanol'],audit='Restored missing oil after palm, corroborated by matching Myschool 1987 question transcription.')
yes(206,'A','2-Methylbuta-1,3-diene is isoprene, a conjugated diene. Its molecules can polymerise into polyisoprene, a rubber. The conjugated double bonds make it a suitable rubber monomer.',['https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/14-6-diene-polymers-natural-and-synthetic-rubbers'],question='Which listed monomer can be polymerised to make a synthetic rubber?',options=['2-Methylbuta-1,3-diene','2-Methylbuta-1,2-diene','2-Methylbut-1-ene','2-Methylbut-2-ene'],audit='Corrected spelling and nomenclature hyphenation. Retained source B as an incorrect proposed name, not a repaired real monomer. Restricted claim to a synthetic rubber, since many rubber formulations exist.')
yes(207,'D','Oxidation of a primary alcohol first gives an aldehyde and then a carboxylic acid when oxidation continues. Propan-1-ol therefore gives propanoic acid, retaining its three-carbon chain.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-3-aldehydes-ketones-carboxylic-acids-and-esters','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/14564?filter=oldest'],question='Complete oxidation of propan-1-ol to its corresponding carboxylic acid gives',options=['Propanal','Propan-2-al','Propan-1-one','Propanoic acid'],audit='Recovered malformed B propan-2-L as printed proposed name propan-2-al from matching Myschool 1987 item14564. B/C remain deliberately incorrect proposed names; no new distractor invented.')
no(208,'The source sentence stops at the gas produced is passed called and, omitting burning/oxygen apparatus conditions. Acetylene from CaC₂ is established but C oxyacetylene flame requires the missing oxygen-combustion premise. Matching older PDFs repeat the omission; recover the full original before publication.',answer='C if burned with oxygen')
no(209,'Essential heated-tube diagram is absent from the normalized record. C is conventional sublimation shorthand, but NH₄Cl vapour involves reversible dissociation/recombination and deposition is not the solid-to-gas step. Need restored accessible apparatus and precise source-compatible wording before teaching the mechanism.',answer='C intended process label')
no(210,'Actual source is 1989 page23, not 1988. C broad melting range supports mixture, but option D has missing verb (solid of the moisture...) and B has suspect density unit. Need full original alternatives instead of guessing absorbs/adsorbs or a unit repair.',year=1989,answer='C')
yes(211,'C','The combining capacities must balance. Four atoms of a trivalent element provide 4×3 = 12 valence units; three atoms of a tetravalent element provide 3×4 = 12. The simplest ratio is therefore M₄X₃.',[RX],question='A compound forms between trivalent metal M and tetravalent non-metal X. What is its simplest formula?',options=['MX','M₃X₄','M₄X₃','M₃X₂'],audit='Changed reversed-valency old B to C. Verified the simplest whole-number ratio without assuming the formula from the old answer.',check='valency-ratio')
yes(212,'D','The empirical formula from C:H = 1:2 is CH₂, with mass 14. The vapour amount is 0.032/22.4 mol, giving molar mass 0.12/(0.032/22.4) = 84 g mol⁻¹. Since 84/14 = 6, the molecular formula is C₆H₁₂.',[RX],year=1989,question='A volatile compound has carbon-to-hydrogen mole ratio 1:2. A 0.12 g sample gives 32 cm³ of vapour at STP. What is its molecular formula? [Molar gas volume = 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹; C = 12, H = 1]',options=['C₃H₆','C₄H₈','C₅H₁₀','C₆H₁₂'],audit='Restored actual 1989 source year from page23 heading, recovered C merged into B, and corrected old C key to D.',check='molecular-formula')
no(213,'Source omits the copper yield of the first 2.25 g oxide; only second2.50g→2.0g is given. Filo has a student/AI reconstruction with first yield2.0g, but no independently reliable original corroborates it. If both yields are2.0g, oxygen masses0.25:0.50 support multiple proportions C. Do not insert a missing measurement based on expected law.',answer='C conditional on first yield2.0g')
no(214,'Actual1989 page23 graph is essential and not present; A and B lack liquid labels. Visual graph has liquidIII reaching the reference pressure at greatest temperature, suggesting C, but complete accessible graph and original A/B are needed before intake.',year=1989,answer='C provisional graph reading')
yes(215,'B','Complete combustion follows C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. At STP, water is condensed, so the gaseous product counted is 3 mol CO₂. Its volume is 3×22.4 = 67.2 dm³.',[RX],question='One mole of propane burns completely in five moles of oxygen. What volume of gaseous product remains after cooling to STP and condensing the water? [Molar gas volume = 22.4 dm³ mol⁻¹]',options=['112.0 dm³','67.2 dm³','56.0 dm³','44.8 dm³'],audit='Corrected role/mole and soap/STP, supported by printed STP molar volume. Made the gas-volume and condensed-water convention explicit; no new numerical premise.',check='propane-volume')
no(216,'Combined gas law gives V₂=0.9×(364/273)×(1/2)=0.600dm³. All printed options2.0,4.5,6.0,8.3 disagree; old B4.5 is not supported. Need an original resolving initial-volume or option decimal error.',answer='0.600dm³, none listed')
no(217,'PDFpage20 graph plots volume against Kelvin temperature as a straight line through origin, so Charles law B, not old Gay-Lussac D. Essential graph and constant-pressure context must be restored. The next Q7 stem was merged into D and also needs private disentangling.',answer='B')
yes(218,'D','Naphthalene is a molecular solid. Its non-polar molecules attract one another mainly through London dispersion forces, a type of van der Waals force. Melting overcomes these intermolecular attractions without breaking the covalent bonds within each molecule.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-1-intermolecular-forces'],question='Naphthalene crystals melt when heated to about 354 K. Which forces between its molecules are overcome during melting?',options=['Coulombic forces between ions','Ionic bonds','Covalent bonds','Van der Waals forces'])
yes(219,'D','Across a period, the number of protons increases while added electrons occupy the same main shell. The increasing effective nuclear attraction generally pulls electrons closer, decreasing atomic radius.',[PERIODIC],question='Which property generally decreases from left to right across a period?',options=['Ionisation energy','Electron affinity','Electronegativity','Atomic radius'],audit='Clarified across as across a period and included general-trend qualifier, not an exception-free claim.')
yes(220,'A','Atomic number 17 identifies chlorine. It has oxidation state −1 in chlorides and +7 in perchlorates such as ClO₄⁻, where x + 4(−2) = −1 gives x = +7.',[RX],question='Which pair gives common oxidation states of the element with atomic number 17?',options=['−1 and +7','−1 and +6','−3 and +5','−2 and +6'],audit='Restored atomic-number wording and explicit positive signs; refers to common compound oxidation states.',check='chlorine-states')
yes(221,'C','Electron affinity describes the energy change when an electron is added to an isolated gaseous atom to form an anion. Ionisation energy instead concerns removing an electron; electronegativity concerns attraction for bonding electrons.',[PERIODIC],question='What is the energy change associated with adding an electron to an isolated gaseous atom called?',options=['First ionisation energy','Second ionisation energy','Electron affinity','Electronegativity'])
no(222,'Printed oxygen:nitrogen atmospheric ratio4:1 is reversed, and dissolved ratio2:1 is also inconsistent with ordinary dissolved air. The likely intended nitrogen:oxygen ratios cannot be substituted solely from expected A; matching SchoolNGR12582 repeats the defect. Need original wording or a separately labelled adapted exercise.',answer='A intended relative-solubility explanation')
