yes(224,'A','Water lost is 1.34−0.71 = 0.63 g, or 0.63/18 = 0.035 mol. Anhydrous Na₂SO₄ has molar mass 142, so 0.71 g is 0.005 mol. The water-to-salt ratio is 0.035/0.005 = 7, giving Na₂SO₄·7H₂O.',[RX],question='Heating 1.34 g of hydrated sodium sulfate leaves 0.71 g of anhydrous Na₂SO₄. What is the hydrate formula? [Na = 23, S = 32, O = 16, H = 1]',options=['Na₂SO₄·7H₂O','Na₂SO₄·3H₂O','Na₂SO₄·2H₂O','Na₂SO₄·H₂O'],audit='Corrected old B to A from mass loss and mole ratio. This is the stated sample, not a claim that all sodium sulfate hydrate is heptahydrate.',check='sulfate-hydrate')
yes(225,'A','Magnesium ions form insoluble salts with soap anions and prevent easy lather formation. Water that lathers readily therefore contains very little Mg²⁺ compared with hard water. Potassium and the listed carbonate species do not themselves cause this soap scum.',['https://edu.rsc.org/experiments/what-ions-cause-hardness-in-water/1788.article'],question='Which listed ion is expected to have a very low concentration in water that lathers readily with soap?',options=['Mg²⁺','K⁺','CO₃²⁻','HCO₃⁻'],audit='Restored carbonate subscript and hydrogen-carbonate charge from source ion context; removed misleading absolute absence claim by retaining very-low-concentration meaning.')
no(226,'HCl is intended, but concentrations are unspecified, so dilute strong and weak acids cannot be ranked unconditionally. Source also labels four optionsA,B,D,E with noC. Retain held until concentration comparison is defined; do not silently add equal concentrations as if source-given.',answer='HCl at comparable concentrations')
yes(227,'D','NaCl is formed from a strong acid and a strong base. Its ions do not appreciably hydrolyse in water, so its solution is approximately neutral. NH₄Cl and FeCl₃ give acidic solutions, while Na₂CO₃ gives an alkaline solution.',[RX],question='Which compound forms an aqueous solution that is neutral to litmus?',options=['NH₄Cl','Na₂CO₃','FeCl₃','NaCl'],audit='Corrected neutralize litmus to neutral to litmus, preserving the standard indicator question.')
yes(228,'D','Complete neutralisation requires three moles of NaOH per mole H₃PO₄: H₃PO₄ + 3NaOH → Na₃PO₄ + 3H₂O. NaOH amount is 0.0450×0.2 = 0.0090 mol, so acid amount is 0.0030 mol. Its volume is 0.0030/0.1 = 0.0300 dm³ = 30.0 cm³.',[RX,'https://www.schoolngr.com/classroom/chemistry/12462'],question='What volume of 0.1 mol dm⁻³ H₃PO₄ is required for complete neutralisation with 45.0 cm³ of 0.2 mol dm⁻³ NaOH?',options=['10.0 cm³','20.0 cm³','27.0 cm³','30.0 cm³'],audit='Recovered missing oxygen subscript4 from matching SchoolNGR12462 question. Corrected old A to D using three acidic hydrogens; stated complete neutralisation convention.',check='phosphate-neutralization')
yes(229,'B','A basic salt retains hydroxide groups from incomplete neutralisation of a base. Mg(OH)Cl contains both OH⁻ and Cl⁻, balancing Mg²⁺. This structural meaning differs from simply giving an alkaline solution, as Na₂CO₃ does.',[RX,'https://www.chem.tamu.edu/class/fyp/mote/ch10-m.html'],question='Which compound is a basic salt containing hydroxide as well as another anion?',options=['Na₂CO₃','Mg(OH)Cl','NaHCO₃','K₂SO₄·Al₂(SO₄)₃·24H₂O'],audit='Reordered source NaCHO₃ to conventional NaHCO₃ without changing its atom counts. Defined basic salt to distinguish partial-neutralisation salts from salts with basic pH.',check='basic-salt-charge')
no(230,'SO₂ can both oxidise and reduce, but H₂ also reduces oxygen and oxidises reactive metals to hydrides; carbon also shows both directions. H₂S sulfur is already−2, contrary to old C justification. Without restricting species/conditions or oxidation centre, there is more than one defensible answer.',answer='B conventional, but A/D can also qualify')
yes(231,'A','Reduction occurs at the cathode. Copper(II) ions accept two electrons each to form copper metal: Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. Chloride oxidation occurs at the anode, and copper dissolution is oxidation rather than cathodic reduction.',[EL],question='Which half-reaction describes copper deposition at the cathode during electrolysis of aqueous copper(II) chloride?',options=['Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)','2Cl⁻(aq) → Cl₂(g) + 2e⁻','Cu(s) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻','Cu²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) → CuCl₂(aq)'],audit='Restored charges/arrows and conventional electron placement. Made copper deposition explicit, preserving electrode-reaction task.',check='copper-cathode')
yes(232,'D','Dissolving solid NaOH in water releases heat to the surroundings. The process is exothermic, so its enthalpy of solution is negative. A positive enthalpy would instead mean heat absorption.',['https://www.eursc.eu/Syllabuses/2019-01-D-46-en-2.pdf'],question='Which statement about dissolving solid NaOH in water is true?',options=['The process is endothermic','The enthalpy of solution is positive','The dissolving NaOH absorbs heat from the surroundings','The enthalpy of solution is negative'],audit='The standalone alternatives concern NaOH dissolution and make no graph reference. An adjacent generic energy graph on sourcepage21 is not needed for this question; no graph-reading claim is made.')
yes(233,'B','A catalyst provides a reaction pathway with lower activation energy. It speeds both directions of a reversible reaction without changing the enthalpy change, free-energy change or equilibrium position.',['https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis'],question='Which property of a reversible reaction is changed by a catalyst?',options=['Reaction enthalpy','Activation energy','Reaction free-energy change','Equilibrium position'],audit='Removed page marker and clarified heat content as reaction enthalpy.')
yes(234,'B','Carbon dioxide is used in CO₂ fire extinguishers. It does not burn and can reduce the oxygen concentration around a suitable fire. The other listed gases are not the extinguishing agent in this type of extinguisher.',['https://www.osha.gov/etools/evacuation-plans-procedures/emergency-standards/portable-extinguishers/about'],question='Which listed gas is used as the extinguishing agent in some fire extinguishers?',options=['Carbon monoxide','Carbon dioxide','Sulfur dioxide','Ammonia'],audit='Expanded oxide names and avoided implying CO₂ is appropriate for every fire.')
no(235,'Yellow Fe³⁺ is reduced to green Fe²⁺, while H₂S is oxidised to sulfur. Source D incorrectly says Fe³⁺ reduced to Fe³⁺; C calls H₂S ions and describes the coupled oxidation. Repairing D alone leaves two substantively true statements without a direct-colour qualifier. Need original alternatives/wording, not an inferred key repair.',answer='D if restored to Fe²⁺ with direct-colour qualifier')
no(236,'CO collection over water is shown in the essential sourcepage22 diagram, absent from the record. C is intended because CO is only sparingly soluble, but the option says absolutely insoluble. Restore accurate accessible apparatus and low-solubility wording before intake.',answer='C')
no(237,'Source equation C₅H₁₀O₅→6C+5H₂O violates carbon conservation. Changing reactant to C₆H₁₀O₅ or product coefficient to5 would both balance but represent different substrates. Matching olderPDF repeats it. C dehydration is likely, but do not invent which formula the exam intended.',answer='C intended; equation unresolved')
no(238,'Standard preparation uses sodium nitrite plus ammonium chloride (A), not old nitrate B. However source A/D use malformed trioxonitrate(III) names, and D already contains ammonium nitrite plus sodium chloride, which can also release N₂ on warming. Need original reagent alternatives and preparation conditions for a unique complete question.',answer='A conventional, D also problematic')
no(239,'Thermal decomposition produces NO₂ (C), but both A and B are printed nitrogen(II) oxide. Recover the duplicate distractor from a trustworthy original. The correct chemistry alone does not recover the complete item.',answer='C')
yes(240,'B','In the chlor-alkali process, concentrated sodium chloride solution (brine) is electrolysed. Chloride ions are oxidised at the anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. This produces chlorine gas.',[EL,'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/18-11-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-halogens'],question='Which process is used for commercial production of chlorine?',options=['Electrolysis of dilute hydrochloric acid','Electrolysis of brine','Neutralisation of hydrogen chloride','Heating potassium chlorate'],audit='Restored hydrogen chloride name and chlorate terminology; retained commercial process distinction.',check='chloride-anode')

